      "updated_at": "2026-08-06T06:12:29Z",
      "body": "Capacity-law follow-on result (run at 02:30): enabling scaled k0 (α=0.45) on the 2.11M corpus took the cover from 14 → 25 regions exactly as designed, but Rule 1+2 came out at **26.1%, identical to baseline**, bits 22.0948 vs 22.1317. Reason: 393,851 of 402,802 held-out positions resolve as ExactContext and only 8,635 (2.1%) ever touch the graph path — fixing the cover cannot move a headline the cover barely participates in. **The cover collapse was real but is not the cause of the scale regression.** The binding constraint is the fixed 4×256 graded code space diluting as records grow, which #446 M1 then confirmed from the other side (17.1× cell subdivision by right context gains +4.6pp on exactly the diluted population). Recommend narrowing this issue to the capacity law itself (shipped, default-off, PR #445) and tracking code-space capacity separately.",
